# Line-ending normalization.
#
# Without this, files committed from Windows (or generated by tools that
# default to CRLF) keep their \r\n endings. That breaks:
#   - Shell scripts: the kernel's #! parser reads `bash\r` as the
#     interpreter name and fails with "No such file or directory".
#   - Helm chart templates: helm tolerates CRLF in YAML but some
#     templated text (especially heredocs in NOTES.txt) renders wrong.
#   - `.dockerignore` / `.gitignore` / `.helmignore`: patterns with a
#     trailing \r don't match the intended paths.
#
# Files committed BEFORE this attributes file landed keep their existing
# line endings until they're rewritten. Run
#   git add --renormalize .
# once to convert the whole tree in a single normalization commit.

# Shell scripts — must be LF.
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Container build inputs.
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.dockerignore text eol=lf

# YAML across the repo: Kubernetes manifests, Helm charts, GitHub
# Actions, Compose files, etc.
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf

# Helm template language (Go text/template inside YAML).
*.tpl       text eol=lf

# Ignore files — pattern matching is whitespace-sensitive.
.gitignore  text eol=lf
.helmignore text eol=lf

# C# / .NET source + project files.
*.cs        text eol=lf
*.cshtml    text eol=lf
*.razor     text eol=lf
*.csproj    text eol=lf
*.props     text eol=lf
*.targets   text eol=lf
*.slnx      text eol=lf
*.sln       text eol=lf
*.resx      text eol=lf

# Python source.
*.py        text eol=lf
*.pyi       text eol=lf

# Rust source.
*.rs        text eol=lf

# Shared build manifest format (Cargo.toml for Rust, pyproject.toml for
# Python, plus anything else that adopts it).
*.toml      text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts: keep CRLF so they run as-expected on Windows
# hosts (PowerShell tolerates either, but the convention is CRLF).
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
